\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\paragraph{Model and Predictor.}
We evaluate on LLaMA-3.1-8B~\cite{grattafiori2024llama3}, which uses SwiGLU FFN blocks~\cite{shazeer2020glu} with $d_{\text{model}}=4096$ and $d_{\text{ff}}=14336$ across 32 layers.
The sparsity predictor is a two-layer MLP with 2.37M parameters per layer (75.9M total), implemented as Linear($4096 \to 128$) + GELU + Linear($128 \to 14336$), trained to produce per-neuron importance scores that minimize output-distribution divergence under sparsity.

\paragraph{Training.}
All predictors are trained for 1000 steps on WikiText-103 with sequence length 2048 and batch size 4, using AdamW~\cite{loshchilov2019adamw} with learning rate $1 \times 10^{-3}$, weight decay $5 \times 10^{-2}$, and cosine decay.
The KL predictor uses Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation~\cite{jang2017gumbel} with temperature $\tau=1.0$ annealed to $0.1$ over training.

\paragraph{Evaluation.}
We report perplexity on WikiText-2~\cite{merity2017pointer} (seq\_len=2048) and downstream accuracy on ARC-Challenge (25-shot), WinoGrande (5-shot), MMLU (5-shot), and GSM8K (8-shot).

\paragraph{Inference Protocol.}
At inference, the predictor produces per-neuron scores that are passed to TEAL's global top-$k$ allocation mechanism~\cite{liu2025teal}, which distributes the sparsity budget across layers non-uniformly.
We term this \textit{predictor-TEAL}: the predictor replaces TEAL's magnitude-based scoring while retaining its allocation strategy.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare against: (1) \textbf{Vanilla TEAL}~\cite{liu2025teal}, which uses activation magnitudes directly; (2) \textbf{BCE predictor} (DejaVu-style~\cite{liu2023dejavu}), trained with per-neuron binary cross-entropy on magnitude-based oracle masks for 1000 and 2700 steps; (3) \textbf{Uniform allocation}, which distributes the sparsity budget equally across layers; and (4) \textbf{WINA}~\cite{liu2025teal}, an importance-weighted variant.


\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the primary comparison. We recommend uniform allocation for deployment (best downstream preservation); global allocation is analyzed to understand allocation-quality tradeoffs (\S\ref{sec:scaling}).
At 30\% sparsity, KL distillation with global TEAL allocation achieves PPL 8.96$\pm$0.03 (3-seed) and reduces TEAL's degradation from dense (10.97 $\to$ 8.96, an 18\% improvement). % exp_id: kl30_3seed_global
The advantage holds across additional downstream benchmarks (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}).
Uniform allocation at 30\% provides stable downstream performance (MMLU 40.7$\pm$1.3\%, 3-seed) while global top-$k$ exhibits high seed variance (30.3$\pm$7.1\%), with late-layer starvation (L31 retention 28.4\%) interacting with predictor initialization (\S\ref{sec:scaling}). % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, kl30_3seed

At 50\% sparsity, KL distillation over vanilla TEAL widens the PPL advantage: 11.79 vs.\ 23.31, a 49\% reduction. % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, bench_teal50
The predictor generalizes to C4 validation at both 30\% (PPL 16.05) and 50\% (52.5\% over TEAL; Appendix~\ref{app:ood}).
However, all methods exhibit downstream collapse on MMLU (28.1$\pm$0.2\% vs.\ 28.0$\pm$0.6\%, both $\approx$ random, 3-seed) and GSM8K ($<$2.2\%); this is a capacity-limited regime where no allocation strategy can recover. % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, d030_kl50_uniform, multi_seed_kl50_global

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Main results on LLaMA-3.1-8B. PPL$\downarrow$ on WikiText-2; accuracy$\uparrow$ on downstream tasks. \textbf{Bold}: best among compensation-free methods per sparsity level. BCE follows the DejaVu~\cite{liu2023dejavu} predictor pipeline (per-neuron BCE on magnitude oracles) with a FastForward-style~\cite{gautam2026fastforward} compensation network (67.2M params) applied post-masking; no TEAL allocation. Entries with $\pm$ report 3-seed mean$\pm$std ($s \in \{42, 123, 456\}$); 50\% uniform PPL is 3-seed ($11.92 \pm 0.07$; Appendix~\ref{app:stability}); remaining entries use seed 42.}
\label{tab:main}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & Method & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
0\% & Dense & 6.12 & 58.28 & 78.06 & 65.27 & 48.75 \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{30\%}
& TEAL vanilla & 10.97 & 42.32 & 66.06 & 38.61 & 7.88 \\ % exp_id: bench_teal30
& KL global (Ours) & 8.96$\pm$0.03 & \textbf{43.9$\pm$3.9} & \textbf{70.6$\pm$0.7} & 30.3$\pm$7.1 & \textbf{8.9$\pm$0.2} \\ % exp_id: kl30_3seed_global
& KL uniform & \textbf{8.82} & 40.78 & 68.59 & \textbf{40.7$\pm$1.3} & 7.35 \\ % exp_id: kl30_3seed_uniform
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{50\%}
& TEAL vanilla & 23.31 & 28.41 & 53.04 & \textbf{28.32} & 1.97 \\ % exp_id: bench_teal50
& KL global (Ours) & \textbf{11.79} & \textbf{32.85} & \textbf{63.46} & 28.1$\pm$0.2 & 1.29 \\ % exp_id: multi_seed_kl50_global
& KL uniform & 11.96 & 32.76 & 62.90 & 28.0$\pm$0.6 & \textbf{2.12} \\ % exp_id: multi_seed_kl50_uniform
& BCE (2700 steps)$^\ast$ & 28.13$\pm$0.20 & 27.6$\pm$0.6 & 51.8$\pm$0.6 & 26.1$\pm$0.5 & 2.1$\pm$0.2 \\ % exp_id: bce50_3seed
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}


\subsection{Extended Training Analysis}
\label{sec:isocompute}

To test whether BCE simply requires more compute, we train it for 2700 steps (2.71$\times$ the KL step budget, approximately equal wall-clock time: $\sim$46 min BCE vs.\ $\sim$45 min KL on RTX PRO 6000).
The BCE predictor achieves PPL 28.13$\pm$0.20 (3-seed), still 2.39$\times$ worse than KL at 1000 steps (PPL 11.79). % exp_id: bce50_3seed
The difference is systematic, not due to architectural or compute limitations: both predictors use identical architectures and TEAL allocation, yet BCE achieves +7pp oracle F1 (0.613 vs.\ 0.540) with 139\% worse PPL (28.13 vs.\ 11.79). KL's near-random precision (0.51) is consistent with learning a mask optimized for distributional fidelity rather than magnitude matching. % exp_id: f1_oracle_analysis, bce_isocompute_s42
Coupled training correlates with better output preservation, but disentangling gradient coupling from oracle quality remains open (BCE uses magnitude oracles while KL optimizes output directly; an ablation using KL-derived masks as BCE targets would isolate the effect).


\subsection{Ablation Studies}

Table~\ref{tab:ablation} isolates the contribution of each design choice at 50\% sparsity.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Ablation at 50\% sparsity on LLaMA-3.1-8B (WikiText-2 PPL$\downarrow$, unified benchmark protocol).}
\label{tab:ablation}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Configuration & PPL$\downarrow$ & $\Delta$ from Ours \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{KL-based variants}} \\
Forward KL + Gumbel (Ours) & \textbf{11.79} & --- \\ % exp_id: bench_kl50_global
JSD (symmetric) + Gumbel & 12.54 & +0.75 \\ % exp_id: jsd_ablation_s42
Reverse KL + Gumbel & 12.80 & +1.01 \\ % exp_id: reverse_kl_ablation_s42
Forward KL + STE & 15.77 & +3.98 \\ % exp_id: ste_baseline_s42
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{BCE-based variants}} \\
BCE + Compensation (hard mask) & 25.44 & +13.65 \\ % exp_id: bce_comp_staged_s42
BCE (no compensation) & 27.97 & +16.18 \\ % exp_id: eval_bce_v3_s42
BCE + Gumbel + Penalty (no KL) & 38.02 & +26.23 \\ % exp_id: bce_gumbel_lagrangian_isolation
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Gumbel vs.\ STE.}
Replacing Gumbel-Sigmoid with STE~\cite{bengio2013ste} degrades PPL from 11.79 to 15.77 (+3.98), accounting for 34.5\% of the improvement over vanilla TEAL. % exp_id: ste_baseline_s42

\paragraph{Divergence Direction.}
Forward KL (mode-covering) outperforms JSD (+6.4\%) and reverse KL (+8.6\%), consistent with the SNR analysis in \S\ref{sec:method:gradient}: complete teacher supervision is associated with better global allocation quality. % exp_id: jsd_ablation_s42, reverse_kl_ablation_s42
This contrasts with MiniLLM~\cite{gu2024minillm} (reverse KL for generation); in mask prediction, mode-covering ensures no important neuron is omitted.

\paragraph{SPON Contribution.}
Adding SPON~\cite{xu2026spon} bias correction reduces PPL from 12.08 to 11.79 (2.4\% relative); see \S\ref{sec:spon} for decomposition.\footnote{SPON analysis uses \texttt{evaluate.py}; main results use the unified benchmark.} % exp_id: spon_2048_s42, mvp_kl_norm_v2_s42

\paragraph{Loss Function Isolation.}
BCE with identical Gumbel-Sigmoid and penalty yields PPL 38.02, worse than vanilla BCE (27.97): the penalty interferes with per-neuron optimization without global coordination. % exp_id: bce_gumbel_lagrangian_isolation
The loss effect ($\Delta$PPL=26.23) is $6.6\times$ the relaxation effect ($\Delta$PPL=3.98).

\paragraph{BCE + Compensation.}
FastForward-style compensation~\cite{gautam2026fastforward} improves BCE from 27.97 to 25.44 (9.0\%), still $2.16\times$ worse than KL. % exp_id: bce_comp_staged_s42

\paragraph{Hyperparameter Robustness.}
Appendix~\ref{app:db_sensitivity} confirms robustness to predictor capacity ($d_b$, $<$3\%), temperature ($<$1.8\%), and training steps (saturated by 800). % exp_id: db_sensitivity_sweep, tau_schedule_sweep, training_steps_sweep


\subsection{Sparsity Scaling and Regime Analysis}
\label{sec:scaling}

Table~\ref{tab:scaling} and Figure~\ref{fig:sparsity_sweep} show PPL as a function of sparsity for the KL predictor under global allocation and vanilla TEAL.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_sparsity_sweep.pdf}
\caption{Perplexity scaling with sparsity. The KL predictor's advantage grows disproportionately as more neurons are pruned.}
\label{fig:sparsity_sweep}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{PPL scaling with sparsity (WikiText-2). KL advantage grows disproportionately with sparsity.}
\label{tab:scaling}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & KL (Ours) & TEAL vanilla & Relative advantage \\
\midrule
30\% & \textbf{8.96} & 10.97 & 18\% \\ % exp_id: kl30_3seed_global
50\% & \textbf{11.79} & 23.31 & 49\% \\ % exp_id: bench_kl50_global
70\% & \textbf{17.91} & 100.11 & 82\% \\ % exp_id: kl_70_global_teal
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The KL advantage grows from 18\% at 30\% to 82\% at 70\%, where TEAL collapses (PPL 100.11) while the learned predictor maintains coherent allocation.
In absolute terms, KL/Dense degradation is $1.47\times$ at 30\%, $1.93\times$ at 50\%, and $2.93\times$ at 70\%, compared to TEAL/Dense ratios of $1.79\times$, $3.81\times$, and $16.4\times$ respectively.

\paragraph{Three Operating Regions.}
Table~\ref{tab:phase} presents global vs.\ uniform allocation at three sparsity levels and reveals qualitative shifts in allocation preference. Denser sampling (e.g., 35\% sparsity) would be needed to precisely locate region boundaries.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Allocation analysis: global (TEAL) vs.\ uniform per-layer allocation across sparsity levels. Entries with $\pm$ report 3-seed mean$\pm$std; remaining entries use seed 42. Each row uses a predictor trained at the respective sparsity target.}
\label{tab:phase}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & Allocation & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
0\% & Dense & 6.12 & 58.28 & 78.06 & 65.27 & 48.75 \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{30\%}
& TEAL vanilla & 10.97 & 42.32 & 66.06 & 38.61 & 7.88 \\ % exp_id: bench_teal30
& KL global & 8.96$\pm$0.03 & \textbf{43.9$\pm$3.9} & \textbf{70.6$\pm$0.7} & 30.3$\pm$7.1 & \textbf{8.9$\pm$0.2} \\ % exp_id: kl30_3seed_global
& KL uniform & \textbf{8.82} & 40.78 & 68.59 & \textbf{40.7$\pm$1.3} & 7.35 \\ % exp_id: kl30_3seed_uniform
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{40\%}
& KL global & 10.12 & 40.96 & 65.35 & \textbf{38.06} & \textbf{5.00} \\ % exp_id: bench_kl40_global
& KL uniform & \textbf{8.36} & \textbf{41.81} & \textbf{67.17} & 30.68 & 3.94 \\ % exp_id: d030_kl40_uniform
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{50\%}
& TEAL vanilla & 23.31 & 28.41 & 53.04 & \textbf{28.32} & 1.97 \\ % exp_id: bench_teal50
& KL global & \textbf{11.79} & \textbf{32.85} & \textbf{63.46} & 28.1$\pm$0.2 & 1.29 \\ % exp_id: multi_seed_kl50_global
& KL uniform & 11.96 & 32.76 & 62.90 & 28.0$\pm$0.6 & \textbf{2.12} \\ % exp_id: multi_seed_kl50_uniform
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

The allocation preference is \emph{non-monotonic}. We observe three qualitative behaviors at the sampled sparsity levels (7--8B scale):

\noindent\textbf{Budget-surplus ($\leq$30\%).}
Uniform allocation provides $5.5\times$ smaller cross-seed MMLU standard deviation ($\sigma$: 1.3pp vs.\ 7.1pp), eliminating catastrophic seed-dependent degradation under global top-$k$ (MMLU 30.3$\pm$7.1\% vs.\ 40.7$\pm$1.3\%, 3-seed). Global top-$k$ starves late layers (L31: 28.4\%, std/mean$=$1.89), and this starvation interacts unpredictably with predictor initialization (Appendix~\ref{app:lagrangian}). % exp_id: kl30_3seed

\noindent\textbf{Budget-constrained ($\sim$40\%).}
The preference reverses: global achieves MMLU 38.06\% vs.\ uniform's 30.68\% (+7.4pp). % exp_id: bench_kl40_global, d030_kl40_uniform
PPL remains low (8.36) but downstream accuracy degrades; PPL is a poor proxy for reasoning.

\noindent\textbf{Capacity-limited ($\geq$50\%).}
Global/uniform gap vanishes (MMLU 28.1$\pm$0.2\% vs.\ 28.0$\pm$0.6\%, $\sigma\leq$0.6pp; contrast 7.1pp at 30\%), consistent with capacity limitation. % exp_id: multi_seed_kl50_global, multi_seed_kl50_uniform
Late-layer starvation persists across levels (L24--31 retention: 50.1/34.2/22.6\% at 30/50/70\% sparsity; Figure~\ref{fig:retention}), consistent on both 7--8B models (\S\ref{sec:cross_arch}).
Under uniform allocation, 53\% of the Dense$\to$KL gap is predictor approximation vs.\ 47\% budget allocation (Appendix~\ref{app:db_sensitivity}).


\subsection{SPON Orthogonality Analysis}
\label{sec:spon}

At 50\% sparsity, the predictor's hidden-state perturbation (L2=482.65) and SPON's additive correction (L2=76.2, cosine similarity 0.196) are weakly correlated ($R^2$=0.038); SPON yields only 2.4\% PPL improvement (12.08$\to$11.79); the KL predictor already captures most recoverable signal. % exp_id: spon_decomposition, spon_2048_s42, mvp_kl_norm_v2_s42

\paragraph{Cross-Architecture Validation.}
\label{sec:cross_arch}
Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch} evaluates on Mistral-7B-v0.3~\cite{jiang2023mistral} (GQA + sliding window, dense PPL 5.52).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Cross-architecture comparison (WikiText-2 PPL$\downarrow$). Adv = TEAL\,/\,KL ratio. LLaMA uses global top-$k$; Mistral uses uniform allocation (matched within each architecture).}
\label{tab:cross_arch}
\small
\begin{tabular}{l@{\hspace{4pt}}cc@{\hspace{3pt}}c@{\hspace{6pt}}|@{\hspace{6pt}}cc@{\hspace{3pt}}c}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{Mistral-7B} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{LLaMA-8B} \\
 & KL & TEAL & Adv & KL & TEAL & Adv \\
\midrule
30\% & \textbf{6.77} & 8.38 & 1.24$\times$ & \textbf{8.96} & 10.97 & 1.22$\times$ \\ % exp_id: kl_30_mistral, teal_30_mistral, kl30_3seed_global
50\% & \textbf{8.65} & 18.31 & 2.12$\times$ & \textbf{11.79} & 23.31 & 1.98$\times$ \\ % exp_id: kl_50_mistral, teal_50_mistral
70\% & \textbf{13.33} & 61.72 & 4.63$\times$ & \textbf{17.91} & 100.11 & 5.59$\times$ \\ % exp_id: kl_70_mistral, teal_70_mistral
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The KL advantage ratio is consistent across architectures (within 8\% at $\leq$50\% sparsity; 4.63$\times$ vs.\ 5.59$\times$ at 70\%) and provides preliminary evidence of generality at the 7--8B scale.

\paragraph{Scale Generalization: Qwen-2.5-14B.}
\label{sec:14b}
Table~\ref{tab:14b} evaluates Qwen-2.5-14B~\cite{qwen2024qwen25} at 30\%: KL's PPL advantage generalizes ($1.47\times$ dense) under both allocations, but MMLU falls below TEAL/BCE regardless (63.7/64.5 vs.\ 71.8/66.3\%), and uniform recovers only +0.8pp. Unlike 8B, the gap appears intrinsic to KL's output-preservation objective rather than allocation-induced. BCE with native allocation collapses to 6.4\% sparsity (\S\ref{sec:method:gradient}); Table~\ref{tab:14b} uses forced global top-$k$. % exp_id: qwen14b_kl30_s42, qwen14b_bce30_s42, qwen14b_bce30_s42_v2, qwen14b_teal30_eval, qwen14b_kl30_uniform, qwen14b_teal30_uniform

